WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.060
Think about what I just said.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.000
Think about how far that actually goes.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.020
This industry sells vanilla ice cream

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.440
And it yells it in a place
where nobody hears it,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.670
that's what this is all about.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.230
This whole week is about
selling vanilla ice cream

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.434
and selling it to a
room that doesn't exist.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:18.748
(dramatic tone)

00:00:18.748 --> 00:00:19.950
(applause)

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:20.783
Hey guys.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:23.663
Let's,

00:00:23.663 --> 00:00:25.390
that's a hardcore setup, so I'll take it.

00:00:26.465 --> 00:00:27.680
I'm really excited to do this.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.540
This is the only consistent thing

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.310
that I've been doing at Cannes, you know,

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.000
for me, unlike a lot of you,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.410
I didn't really grow up
aspiring to be in the industry,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.270
and didn't spend the first 35 years

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.490
of my life in the industry,

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.713
I'll try to speak up
'cause I know it's loud.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.470
So just to give you context,

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.090
'cause I'm sure most or
half of you don't know,

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.300
I run a company called VaynerMedia.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.450
I started it ten years
ago with my brother AJ.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.790
My career, as an entrepreneur, I grew up,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.430
I was born in the Soviet Union,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.940
I came to the US when I was a little kid,

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.090
very immigrant kind of upbringing.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:10.923
Let's go.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.930
Yep, (speaks in foreign language).

00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:15.484
- [Audience Member] (murmurs question)

00:01:15.484 --> 00:01:16.317
- Yes.

00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:17.150
Mic?

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.134
- Yeah,--
- Or do you wanna wait?

00:01:18.134 --> 00:01:18.967
- We're gonna get you a mic.
- Okay, no worries.

00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:20.810
- So just wait 'cause we don't wanna miss

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.320
all this amazing content.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.153
- No worries.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.821
Should we do Q and A?

00:01:26.821 --> 00:01:27.867
(laughter)

00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:29.212
Like, real quick.

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:30.045
- [Audience Member]
Like, (speaks Russian),

00:01:30.045 --> 00:01:30.963
when do you move here?

00:01:30.963 --> 00:01:32.974
- Yeah, (speaks Russian), 1978.

00:01:32.974 --> 00:01:34.670
- [Audience Member] Okay,
your Russian is still--

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.970
- I really understand it,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:40.320
'cause I don't use it,
Russian people make fun of me,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.070
but but if I went to Moscow for a week.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:43.903
- [Audience Member] Yeah, exactly.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.250
- If I just heard it enough.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.730
It's really interesting
growing up an immigrant

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:52.540
because you're you're outside
world's this new place

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.740
but the house is still the
old school, you know, right?

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.650
- [Audience Member] That is--

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:57.483
- That is something I don't see,

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.300
you hear it, I've heard
other people talk about it,

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.090
but I don't think it's talked about enough

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.370
and then, more importantly, I wonder

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.100
what that actually means, you know,

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.930
there's gotta be something pretty profound

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.160
about living two different worlds

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.130
and it manifests in such fun inside jokes,

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:18.130
like, for example, I don't
take any medicine ever.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:19.910
It's a hardcore thing in my,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.300
it's very like hardcore Eastern Europe,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.313
it's unbelievable how
much that's in my culture.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.560
And just food, just growing up
in the '80s in America, where

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.870
it's been amazing with the
Whole Foods and the Wegmans

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.980
and just these progressive
food culture in America

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.920
but in the '80s it was like six things.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.980
So my friends would come over,

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.460
I remember nobody in
America, in the '80s or '90s,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:52.010
knew what pomegranate was, like nobody.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.083
But, it was a hardcore Russian thing,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:56.997
people would come to my house and be like,

00:02:56.997 --> 00:02:57.930
"The fuck is?"

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.463
Just literally look at
it for like five minutes.

00:03:03.923 --> 00:03:04.960
Should I go since it got quieter or?

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.040
- [Staff] Yeah, you can--
- Okay.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.350
So there's a lot of things
I wanna talk to you about

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.260
and I wanna do even more Q&amp;A
'cause I think context matters.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.610
But to frame up up the conversation,

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.230
I think what was really cool was

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.450
I was a lemonade stand
kid, I was a shovel snow,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.960
wash cars, like always my true hobby,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.570
to this day I garage sale
and do all these weird things

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:31.180
'cause my true hobby is business.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.490
I genuinely love it,

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.080
I'm sure for some of you
that were probably so happy

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.530
that this was an industry,
'cause you grew up drawing,

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.527
or creating, or whatever like,

00:03:38.527 --> 00:03:39.910
"Oh, wait, that's a job?"

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.730
That's how business is for me.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.190
When I have downtime I'm trying
to start side businesses.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.683
So loved business.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.020
Grew up in an environment where
my dad worked every minute

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.180
to try to make it in the new world

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.640
and he eventually owned a
liquor store in New Jersey.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.320
I was dragged into that when I was 14.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.923
And, really, from day one,

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.740
I started doing two things
that are interesting to me,

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.270
I started doing a lot of UI, UX,

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.500
which in in the way I would say that was

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.540
I started moving my dad's store around.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.050
I used to stand behind the register

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.600
and it wasn't that busy, right,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.610
where I could watch almost
every customer walk in.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.580
And where would they go?

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:20.413
And what would they buy?

00:04:20.413 --> 00:04:23.230
And intuitive, as 14, I would understand.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:24.760
Like, why do we send them that way,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.340
where we sell stuff that's
not as expensive as over here?

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.010
Really, in hind, a lot of this stuff

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.690
in the last five years I've recalled.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.840
Even lemonade, when I was six years old,

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.350
instead of standing behind
my own lemonade stands,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.980
I got my friends to do it,

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.030
because I would literally
walk up and down the streets

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.200
of New Jersey watching cars drive by

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.290
to try to figure out what tree,

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.400
or what post to put a sign on.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.610
So, I've been truly,
intuitively, chasing attention

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.080
my whole life, as a matter of fact,

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.480
it's gonna bubble up in a minute,

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.220
one of the biggest reasons I push

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.050
against television advertising

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.540
and think it's grossly
overpriced and overrated

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:03.760
is not because I don't
believe in the craft,

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.330
or a 30-second video,
it's that I don't believe

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.630
people are consuming them.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.480
I think that needs to matter.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.980
You could literally make the
greatest thing of all time

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.540
it's kind of like the tree in the forest

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.050
you're gonna spend nine
months making something

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.770
that nobody sees, seems
like a waste of time.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:23.310
So, was that kind of kid.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.310
I'll fast forward 'cause
I wanna get into the meat.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.220
In 1996 I launched one of
the first three e-commerce

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:29.730
wine businesses in America.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.030
My dad had a liquor store,

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.420
we were in a middle-class area,

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.440
but there was affluent towns around us.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.010
I saw people wanted wine.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:38.710
I started reading about wine like crazy,

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.040
got super educated about wine as a 15, 16,

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:45.410
17 year-old kid, super weird in hindsight.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:50.000
Launched in '96, had email
open rates of like 90 percent,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.393
was the first day one advertiser
on Google Search, day one.

00:05:53.393 --> 00:05:56.360
Five cents a click, no, typing in words

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.290
that had nobody type, like Cabernet

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.073
had no bids or, like crazy shit.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.340
Built my dad's business from a three

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.130
to a sixty million dollar
business very quickly.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.310
That changed our immigrant
life as you can imagine.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:14.400
kinda made it, was fancy,
you know, in the wine world.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.410
Whiz kid, articles, AP, all
the things you would want.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.880
And then YouTube came out, and four months

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:24.360
after YouTube came out
I intuitively understood

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.320
it was gonna be big and
I started, legitimately,

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.030
one of the first long-form
shows on YouTube.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.160
Every day, five days a week, 20 minutes,

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.490
me in front of a camera tasting four wines

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:35.770
and talking about it.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:40.770
Completely exploded, like
hardcore web 2.0 phenomenon,

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.780
was a dig.coms and Reddits and Boing Boing

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.680
and just got a lot of coverage.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.850
There was a couple of vlogs.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:52.420
Diggnation, Rocketboom, I
mean you guys are so young.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.320
This was before there
was a video on YouTube

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.020
that had a million views.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.170
A video.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.430
So super early.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.640
Got national attention.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.620
Conan O'Brien had me on his show.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:05.610
The whole fucking Internet
was hitting me up,

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.950
like, "Do it for us."

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:10.090
It was that small of a kind of like,

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.080
it's crazy to even talk
about this right now.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.930
'Cause, first of all,
'cause it's only 2007,

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.040
it's not that long ago.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.750
Go on, it went super viral.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.810
And then I realized that this whole world

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.530
was gonna be huge and that is,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.570
I was super right about e-commerce, email

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:28.030
and Google AdWords and I did great.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.350
But, then I started reading articles

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:33.410
about Google going public,
and YouTube selling

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:35.980
for 1.7 billion dollars, and I was like

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:37.970
this talent I have, I
might be able to use it

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.660
for something more than
selling some more champagne,

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.960
or Bordeaux, and so I decided

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.260
to become an angel investor
in early-stage startups.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:48.770
The first three companies I invested in

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:51.080
were Facebook, Twitter and Tumblr.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.125
Yeah, it's like, you know, yeah.

00:07:53.125 --> 00:07:54.510
(laughter)

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.010
Obviously, that kind of
changed the course of my life.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:59.410
This was all way, I mean,
I bought my Facebook stock

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:02.373
from Mark's parents, like early shit.

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:06.317
That was good, changed
the course of my life.

00:08:06.317 --> 00:08:07.150
"Made it"

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.010
You know, totally different way.

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:10.860
And then I did something interesting.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.230
It's 2009, my brother's about
to graduate from college,

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.260
he's 11 years younger to
me, we're super tight,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.410
we knew we wanted to
do something together.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:23.743
I'm now getting super
attention from family offices,

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:28.475
global billionaires, hedge funds,

00:08:28.475 --> 00:08:31.750
people that want me to raise
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:35.780
to do a fund to invest
'cause "I can pick 'em".

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:37.200
And that was probably the
most interesting point

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.480
of my career, where I
could have easily gone down

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:43.370
the path of extremely, in
my opinion, easy money.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.230
I don't know how much you
know about running a VC firm,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.930
but you get 2% of the
overall money you raise

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.780
just for managing it and
you get 20% on the back end

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:53.200
after you pay it back.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.200
You raise three, four, $500 million,

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.333
you're making a lot of money
a year just to take meetings.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:02.430
And I remember just knowing
in my heart, intuitively,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.810
even though it was extremely attractive,

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.660
I was young, I was 34,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:08.870
that I didn't wanna not be a practitioner.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.460
I'm gonna say something
pretty interesting right now

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.570
which I genuinely think is true.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:13.970
And it's a hyperbolized statement,

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.123
but I think it's true.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:18.790
There's not a single
person at Cannes right now

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.540
that makes more content
on a daily basis than me.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.270
I put out 50 to 100
pieces of content a day,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:25.843
across seven to 10 platforms.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.010
To be the person at this event,

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:31.620
and I don't know, how many
people on earth exist right now

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.320
that produced as much content as I do.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:37.940
I'm posting again 50 to 100
pieces of creative a day,

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:41.960
across Facebook, Instagram,
LinkedIn, Twitter podcasts.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.070
I'm a deep practitioner.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:45.903
Right.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:49.250
And it's fun, some of my
lead creatives are here.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:51.050
It's fun sometimes when we jam.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:52.477
I love reminding my company, I'm like,

00:09:52.477 --> 00:09:54.730
"Look, I'm actually doing this."

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:57.080
And there's something
very powerful about that.

00:09:58.242 --> 00:10:01.250
I'm not ideating and
producing long-form video,

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.090
but I'm pump putting out content.

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.133
And so I knew that that's who I was.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.890
I literally went completely the other way

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:12.810
instead of going down
that huge financial path,

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.630
I decided to build, and
don't get it confused,

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.660
one of the worst businesses you can build,

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:21.140
which is a client service
creative business.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.980
Now let me tell you why I did it.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.370
My ambition 10 years ago stays true today,

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:30.500
I want to buy some of
the most nostalgic brands

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.220
that have ever existed, when
the next economy collapses.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:36.480
So when this economy finally collapses

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.113
I wanna go buy KitKats,
Bubblicious, K-Swiss,

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.400
Ralph Lauren, I wanna buy brands.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.170
And then I want to be the CEO of it.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:49.530
And what I knew then was I
understood small businesses,

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.650
I understood Silicon Valley,

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.370
I didn't understand
fortune 500 businesses.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:56.600
But, I wasn't gonna go get a job.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.770
So I decided to start the
agency because at that point

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.910
I realized marketing was my superpower.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:03.740
Hanging out with all those
Silicon Valley titans,

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.650
why was I invited to the dinner?

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.040
Why were they listening to me?

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.000
It's because they were great a tech,

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:10.320
but I really understood.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.780
One of the biggest reasons,

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:14.210
forget about the current way
people view Mark Zuckerberg

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:17.940
and Facebook, Mark
Zuckerberg's deep understanding

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.400
of human psyche was the number
one reason we connected.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:24.650
I think I'm an extremely good marketer

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.060
because I think this entire thing

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:31.040
that we're at right now is
an internal B2B ecosystem

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.520
and the only thing I pay
attention to is the end consumer.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.263
I know so little about
what's going on here.

00:11:38.506 --> 00:11:40.780
This is literally, the
first three years I was here

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.720
I only did this event, I
didn't know anything else.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:44.784
You guys remember.

00:11:44.784 --> 00:11:46.760
Harry had just joined us to run comms

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:48.620
from BBDL, she's incredible.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:49.780
I'm still asking her.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.920
On the way here she's like,
we were fine, finalists,

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:53.753
what's it called?

00:11:53.753 --> 00:11:54.586
- [Harriet] We get shortlist.

00:11:54.586 --> 00:11:55.880
- Shortlist, she's like
we were shortlisted

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.870
for a bunch of stuff today.
I'm like, I don't even know,

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.450
I literally thought it was finalist.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.283
I still don't.

00:12:00.283 --> 00:12:01.930
Adam, yesterday we won a silver thing

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:02.763
and I was like what does that mean?

00:12:02.763 --> 00:12:07.763
Like I still am so deeply
unaware of what's happening here.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.870
Not out of audacity,
not out of disrespect.

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.610
I actually love coming here, the vibe.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:14.040
I love hanging with you guys.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:17.030
I love this, it's just that
everything I think about

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:17.883
is the consumer.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.270
It's the only thing that matters

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.300
and that's why I believe that,

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:28.210
I'm humbled by the most
interesting, or one of the.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.300
We're doing some of the
most interesting things

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.170
because we just see it different,

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.950
we just see it different and
the way we see it is practical.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:40.290
So I mean, the far majority of
what you've already worked on

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:41.790
you would have never consumed.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:45.660
That's the fucking truth.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.960
And I think that's that's my aspiration

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:50.360
to come to things like this.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.360
Actually have a conversation
about what the hell's going on.

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:57.370
Creativity is amazingly fun.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:12:59.610
I think that we have to be very thoughtful

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:03.320
that somebody's paying us
to make something happen.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:05.660
I think there's a lot of audacity

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:09.080
that seeps into this
craft, where it becomes

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:12.430
about ideology and selfish behavior.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:16.320
I watch creatives and
strategists make decisions

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:17.640
all the time, based on what,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:20.710
they wanna blow up a building,
they wanna meet John Legend,

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.810
they want this joke to see the world.

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:26.120
It has nothing to do with
what the client needs

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.750
and definitely nothing to do with,

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.980
does the end customer care?

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:31.500
And so I think a lot about that.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.670
And so we started VaynerMedia.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:35.680
You know, it's interesting
my aspirations were,

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:37.020
a couple things I didn't know,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:39.980
first of all, the first 45
people that worked for my agency

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:42.063
had zero days of agency experience.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:46.340
I mean you wanna talk about
a a fucking shit show.

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:48.770
We won a million-dollar scope from Pepsi

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:51.207
and on the way out the
guy grabs me and says,

00:13:51.207 --> 00:13:53.497
"Hey", incredibly provocative,

00:13:53.497 --> 00:13:55.063
"You're gonna get the
business this is incredible.

00:13:55.063 --> 00:13:58.243
" This is what we're looking
for." It's 2009 then he goes,

00:14:00.827 --> 00:14:01.770
"One piece of advice."

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.887
I'm like please because
it was so early, he goes,

00:14:03.887 --> 00:14:05.257
"You need to hire somebody who knows

00:14:05.257 --> 00:14:06.457
"how to make a deck."

00:14:07.945 --> 00:14:09.430
I will never forget that.

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:10.850
By the way, it took us another three years

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:12.863
before we actually knew
how to make a deck.

00:14:14.934 --> 00:14:16.980
We are the fastest, organically grown

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.460
to a 100 million-dollar revenue agency

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.640
in the industry's history.

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:26.600
And we win no RFPs, like
we're just incapable

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.240
because we come in with a philosophy,

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:31.520
we're not willing to, you know,
everybody sells on an idea

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:33.000
and it's just not something,

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.930
we have pregnant it, we flirt with it,

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:39.130
but the truth is we have a philosophy.

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.650
Which is a couple things
that we believe in

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:42.483
that I think are unique.

00:14:42.483 --> 00:14:45.900
One, we believe that volume of creative

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:49.250
is the cost of entry of
relevance of the next decade.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:51.650
And that we think this
world needs to understand

00:14:52.565 --> 00:14:55.100
that quantity does not
mean lack of quality,

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:57.343
and, more importantly what is quality?

00:14:58.621 --> 00:15:00.830
For example, I think
the rise of copywriting

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:03.210
is going to be profound
over the next decade

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.010
because I don't think people understand

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.560
that the copy change, literally the copy

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:09.700
that associates every every post

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.090
on Instagram, Facebook, Twitter

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:13.840
and things of this nature
that will continue to grow

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:17.710
in attention is massively important

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:19.210
to the output of success.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:23.180
We become so visual it's,
so video and Instagram

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.240
now that people are mailing in copy

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:27.810
for all of you that have your own ambition

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.906
to grow your Instagram as cop,

00:15:30.906 --> 00:15:32.620
I watch copywriters in my company

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:35.710
who are interested in
growing their Instagram

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:38.300
mail in copy, because they've been sucked

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.590
into how visual the medium is.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:44.460
So volume to us is imperative.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.050
We also think that Facebook and YouTube

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.760
and whatever the Facebook
and YouTube, and Instagram

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:53.390
of the day is, that
internet long-form video

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.780
is actually where brand
building is being done.

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:57.453
Not in the form of TVC.

00:15:58.866 --> 00:16:03.866
I think that we have to have a
conversation in this industry

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:06.993
about things actually being consumed.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:11.870
And I think that common
sense needs to actually

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.310
be part of the conversation.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:15.893
It is 2019.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:21.710
The far majority of people,
all across the globe.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:25.050
are watching OTT at scale.

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:28.730
There is so little network television

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:33.730
being consumed in major 10
markets, U.S., Europe, Asia,

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:37.700
so little, and then for
that person to actually,

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.760
first of all, they're
not watching the show.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:41.110
And if they're even watching the show

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:44.010
for them to serendipitously
watch a commercial,

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.210
it's just not happening.

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:49.100
Watching a commercial is
getting dangerously close

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:50.913
to looking up the yellow pages.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:55.140
And yet we celebrate it
like nobody's business.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.633
And I think we have to challenge that.

00:16:56.633 --> 00:17:01.160
There's no way in your
careers that TV commercials

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.680
are gonna be on the
pedestal, it's impossible.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:06.230
You're basically, you know,
I look at it like this,

00:17:07.516 --> 00:17:08.630
It's a U.S.-centric example,

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:10.220
I know we've got a lot of people here

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:14.390
but every country in the
world has its major retailers

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:17.050
and has its emerging internet retailers.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:22.050
I think this world continues
to celebrate Walmart and Target

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:27.120
and big-box retail while
I keep beating the drum

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:29.710
that Amazon is here and
it's gonna be a problem.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.110
And I think that's the
analogy for creative,

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:35.840
we have to have this conversation.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.253
You can't celebrate something nobody sees.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.123
And so that's important to me.

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:44.960
But, make no mistake, I believe creativity

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:47.070
is the variable of success, right.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:48.500
The creative.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:50.470
All the strategy work which is imperative,

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:51.470
all the other stuff.

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:53.990
We're A fully integrated agency,

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:55.930
we have media and creative under one roof.

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.330
I believe in your career you will see all

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:00.320
of the agencies consolidate
media and creative

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:01.560
under one roof.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:03.670
It is not working for the clients

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:06.540
to have media and
creative in separate rooms

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:09.040
because I'm sure you've
already seen this in meetings

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:11.103
when it doesn't work everybody does this.

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:14.470
The media agency's like,
"It's the fucking creative."

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:15.881
The creative agency's like,

00:18:15.881 --> 00:18:17.660
"Their fucking media plan sucked."

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:18.493
And that's been unbelievably smart

00:18:18.493 --> 00:18:21.650
for the holding companies to separate it

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:23.930
and quite profitable, but the brands

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:25.570
are starting to not be happy.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.350
And they're the ones
who write your checks,

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:28.430
and that will play out.

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:30.360
So I'm excited about that.

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:31.740
I started the agency with 45 people

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:32.680
that didn't work in the industry.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:33.730
I knew nothing about the industry.

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:35.430
I'd read nothing about the industry

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.490
So I started VaynerMedia.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:40.700
I built a creative shop that
I put media in the name.

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:44.070
I had both media and
creative under one roof.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.260
And had no idea that it was unique.

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:49.440
So we were doing all these unusual things.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:51.740
What it led to is

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:54.640
a very large agency

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:57.440
that has not played by the industry rules

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.140
but has driven business
results for its clients.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.160
So now we do have 900 people globally.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:06.430
We do have the U.S., L.A.,
and New York offices,

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:09.190
and London, and opening up Singapore.

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:12.730
And, very frankly, feel
very confident we're about

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:15.060
to get a big scope for
the World Cup in Qatar.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:20.060
And that means Middle East
and looking at South America.

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:23.670
So I think it's gonna be
interesting to see what happens.

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.900
I think because of some of the awards

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:27.140
we're winning this week, just,

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.620
I'm feeling from the creative class here

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:31.527
a little bit of more of a,

00:19:31.527 --> 00:19:32.850
"Okay you can be in the club?"

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:33.977
And I'm laughing I'm like,

00:19:33.977 --> 00:19:36.020
"I don't want to be in the club."

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.000
I don't mind to be in the club

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:39.820
but there's only one club I wanna be in

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:42.740
which is, wouldn't you
want to be a creative

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:44.920
that in real life, when you
hang out with your friends,

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:46.820
people know what you're talking about?

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:50.580
How many more times do people
here wanna spend 20 years

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:52.530
of their life making TVC

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.320
that when they talk about it

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:58.050
and they're the IBM spot and
their friends and relatives

00:19:58.050 --> 00:19:59.700
are like, "I have no idea
what you're talking about."

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:01.930
And if they do it's because
they tried really hard

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:04.253
to find it, right?

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:07.437
I think that people want to,

00:20:07.437 --> 00:20:08.630
and I think if you're creative,

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:10.380
I think you want it to be seen.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:11.790
I just don't know what else to say.

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:14.730
And I think what Mike and Adam
will tell you if you decide

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.660
to yap with them after this,

00:20:16.660 --> 00:20:19.260
is after having careers in fancy places,

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:21.523
doing fancy stuff it's fun to make.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:26.180
Think about all the ideas
that you guys have had

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:28.093
that will never see the day of life.

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:33.330
You guys are all 12 years
away, six years away,

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.050
four years away from being
the final decision-maker

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:37.810
to actually see something made.

00:20:37.810 --> 00:20:42.020
Right now you're
politicking and strategizing

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:44.490
on how to convince the
two people above you

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:46.773
to actually make the thing you believe in.

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:49.880
You're not in the creative industry,

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:52.283
you're in the internal
politicking industry.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:57.740
That's why volume will
set this industry free.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:02.070
We have interns from day one in ad school

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:05.230
being able to see their thoughts in market

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.930
from the biggest brands
in the world, literally.

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:11.180
In terms of, we have
literally interns, already,

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:13.060
in our company that have had more pieces

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:15.550
of content seen by humans
and some of the people here.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:16.640
That's real.

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:20.300
That's going to play out,
I hope, for all of you.

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:21.420
I want you to be in a place

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:24.150
where you get to be creative for real,

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:26.920
instead of working at a bank

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:30.650
and fighting for a half pregnant version

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:33.260
of your idea to see the world.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:36.040
And that's why so many of you
have to do things on the side,

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.790
'cause you can't scratch your itch.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:40.443
- [Taya] First of all,

00:21:40.443 --> 00:21:42.215
I wanted to work for you one day.

00:21:42.215 --> 00:21:43.048
- Good.

00:21:43.048 --> 00:21:44.560
- [Taya] Not too far away.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:46.547
But, I have a question, because I--

00:21:46.547 --> 00:21:47.380
- what's your name?

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:48.213
- [Taya] Taya.

00:21:48.213 --> 00:21:49.550
- Taya?
- Yeah.

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:51.460
I have a question.

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:55.330
My clients have to
research all our TVC work,

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:56.675
and that's the only way

00:21:56.675 --> 00:21:59.020
that they can get their
budgets signed off.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:00.050
So what's your advice?

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:02.620
Because they really
don't care about digital,

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:05.240
or even Shoppa, so what's your advice

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:09.650
to get them to see it in a
new way from a personal use,

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:12.811
yeah, down the ranks, at the moment.

00:22:12.811 --> 00:22:16.740
- They're not seeing it from
a person that's up the ranks.

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:18.430
So here's what I would say,

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:20.310
here's a really, really
important piece of advice

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.120
that I think will bring you value,

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:26.560
please don't stay quiet because
you know it doesn't matter

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:28.630
and you know it won't
play well in the room.

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:29.910
Now, that doesn't mean be like me

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:33.690
and be super combative,
it means be respectful.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:36.340
But let me give you one
humongous piece of advice,

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.450
the world's gonna change,
animatics are the stupidest shit

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:40.283
of all time it.

00:22:40.283 --> 00:22:41.116
(laughter)

00:22:41.116 --> 00:22:43.480
Ipsos is the stupidest shit of all time,

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:45.830
it can't sustain itself and it will break,

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:47.540
and it's getting closer than you think.

00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:50.450
So, it's important for you to share your

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:52.140
actual points of view in these rooms,

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:53.880
'cause people are watching.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.680
And right now you may be frowned upon,

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:59.240
or it's silly, or it's
naivete, or it's a kid talking

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:01.040
but everybody's watching and listening.

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:04.150
And in three years when
that person's fired

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:06.500
and then goes to a new
place and they have to win

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:09.270
in digital they're gonna
be like, "She knew."

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.163
And so die on your sword.

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:14.050
A lot of you are staying quiet

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:15.540
because you know it will help you grow

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:17.780
but you're not, you guys are agreeing

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:19.620
to things you don't actually believe in.

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:22.060
You're doing it because within
the four walls you work in

00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:24.140
that's what you know the rules are.

